Subject: [PATCH 34/34] a PCI bus never declared its cell counts, so every Forth decode read one cell short
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 0.6d. `" screen" open-dev` faulted -- a general protection fault
with dstackcnt=-3 on amd64, an invalid opcode with a throw -4 on x86 --
and patch 33 did not fix it, which is what TODO 0.6d had predicted it
would. The two were independent after all.

CAUSE. #address-cells and #size-cells are written from pci_dev->acells /
->scells (pci.c:1367), and NOTHING in drivers/pci_database.c assigns
either field, on any entry. host_config_cb would have been the other
candidate home and is never reached here at all: QEMU's i440FX is not in
the database -- every boot log carries "Cannot manage 'PCI host bridge'
... 8086 1237" -- so it falls to the generic subclass row, whose
config_cb is NULL.

So the property was never written, and forth/device/property.fs's
my-#acells fell back to its no-property default of TWO for every child of
a PCI bus. Measured at the amd64 prompt under
/pci8086,1237@0/QEMU,VGA@2:

    my-#acells = 2

EVERYTHING ON THE C SIDE ENCODES THREE CELLS REGARDLESS --
pci_encode_phys_addr writes phys.hi/mid/lo -- so `reg` and
`assigned-addresses` were correct on disk the whole time. It is the FORTH
decode side that read two where three were written, and the mismatch is a
silent stack shift rather than an error: drivers/pci.fs's
pci-bar>pci-addr is commented ( reg prop prop-len phys.lo phys.mid
phys.hi ) and receives one fewer, so its `6 pick` reaches past the
operand it wants and its exit path leaves the wrong count. map-fb then
hands pci-map-in four items it may not have, and the machine falls over
in whatever way that arch falls over.

AFTER, on both arches:

    my-#acells            3
    pci-bar>pci-addr      leaves exactly 5:  40000000 0 42001010 1000000 -1
    " screen" open-dev    ih=14dd8 (amd64) / 12a64 (x86)
    frame-buffer-adr      40000000
    exceptions            0

3 and 2 are what the 1275 PCI binding specifies and what every C encoder
here already assumes.

WATCHED TO BITE. The `vga` track asserted only that the alias RESOLVED,
which is not the same as being able to use it -- open-dev faulted on both
arches for as long as that was all it checked. It now asserts the cause
and the effect separately, because they were two bugs: AC (my-#acells is
3), OD (open-dev returns an ihandle), FB (frame-buffer-adr is the BAR
address from patch 33), and that the log carries no exception at all.
Removing these two lines fails it by name:

    REGRESSION: amd64: my-#acells under the VGA node is 2, not 3

Run: vga property-abi mmio-writer pmem-writer flash-writer amd64
multiboot diagnostics ppc nvram amd64-pmem dict-identity client-forth
amd64-ctx -- all 14 PASS. This is shared code and reaches ppc, which is
why the sweep includes it.

diff --git a/drivers/pci.c b/drivers/pci.c
--- a/drivers/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci.c
@@ -1862,6 +1862,42 @@
         } else {
             BIND_NODE_METHODS(phandle, ob_pci_bus_node);
         }
+
+        /* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 0.6d: A PCI BUS MUST DECLARE ITS CELL
+         * COUNTS, and no node in this tree ever did.
+         *
+         * #address-cells / #size-cells are written from pci_dev->acells /
+         * ->scells (pci.c:1367) -- and NOTHING in drivers/pci_database.c assigns
+         * either field, on any entry. host_config_cb would have been the other
+         * candidate home, and it is never reached here at all: QEMU's i440FX is
+         * not in the database (every boot log says "Cannot manage 'PCI host
+         * bridge' ... 8086 1237"), so it falls to the generic subclass row,
+         * whose config_cb is NULL. Hence this sits on the bind path, which every
+         * bridge takes on every arch.
+         *
+         * So the property was never written, and forth/device/property.fs's
+         * my-#acells fell back to its no-property default of TWO for every child
+         * of a PCI bus. Measured at the amd64 prompt under
+         * /pci8086,1237@0/QEMU,VGA@2: my-#acells = 2.
+         *
+         * Everything on the C side encodes three cells regardless
+         * (pci_encode_phys_addr writes phys.hi/mid/lo), so `reg` and
+         * `assigned-addresses` are right on disk. It is the FORTH decode side
+         * that read two where three were written, and the mismatch is a silent
+         * stack shift: drivers/pci.fs's pci-bar>pci-addr is commented
+         * ( reg prop prop-len phys.lo phys.mid phys.hi ) and receives one fewer,
+         * so its `6 pick` reaches past the operand it wants.
+         *
+         * Downstream that is TODO 0.6d: `" screen" open-dev` faults, because
+         * drivers/vga.fs's map-fb calls pci-bar>pci-addr and then hands
+         * pci-map-in four items it may not have. amd64 reported a general
+         * protection fault with dstackcnt=-3, x86 an invalid opcode with a
+         * throw -4 -- the same underflow in local costume.
+         *
+         * 3 and 2 are what the 1275 PCI binding specifies and what every C
+         * encoder here already assumes. */
+        set_int_property(phandle, "#address-cells", 3);
+        set_int_property(phandle, "#size-cells", 2);
         break;
     }
 
